\title{The Polygon / Associahedron Model of the Finite Type $A$ Cluster Algebra of $\Gr(2, m)$}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-25}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The homogeneous coordinate ring of the Grassmannian $\Gr(2, m)$ of $2$-planes in $m$-space, in its Pl\"ucker embedding, is a cluster algebra of finite type $A_{m-3}$. Its cluster combinatorics is governed by a convex $m$-gon: Pl\"ucker coordinates correspond to segments between vertices, the $m$ frozen coordinates to the sides, the mutable coordinates to the diagonals, clusters to triangulations, and mutations to flips. We present a self-contained combinatorial development of this model with three exactly proven cornerstones. First, a convex $m$-gon ($m \ge 3$) has exactly $\tfrac{m(m-3)}{2}$ diagonals, proved in the division-free form $2 \cdot \diagonalCount(m) = m(m-3)$ from a genuine enumeration of diagonals as unordered pairs of vertices. Second, every triangulation of an $m$-gon uses exactly $m - 3$ diagonals, establishing constancy of rank. Third, the set of triangulations is finite of cardinality the Catalan number $C_{m-2}$, via the classical bijection with binary trees having $m - 2$ internal nodes and a constructive enumeration of those trees; equivalently, type $A_r$ has $C_{r+1}$ clusters. We further record that the flip (exchange) graph has finitely many vertices --- the $1$-skeleton of the associahedron $K_{m-1}$. Throughout, the model is positioned as the cornerstone case of the conjecture that a Schubert cell $X_w^\circ$ in a type $A$ flag variety carries a finite-type cluster algebra if and only if $w$ is Grassmannian (has at most one descent).
\end{abstract}

